<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ong Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1203</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Toghrul, Wang Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ong Khan, born Toghrul, was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and a sworn brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>'s father. He supported the young Temüjin, the future Genghis Khan, and received the title Ong (Wang) Khan from the Jin dynasty after a joint campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> in 1196. The two later fell out, and Temüjin defeated him in 1203; he was killed while fleeing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ong-khan.jpg" alt="Ong Khan" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maître d'Egerton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WangKhan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1203 Temüjin defeated <b>Toghrul</b> and used the flight of Toghrul's son through Tangut land as a pretext to raid Western Xia in 1205; in 1206, the year he became Genghis Khan, Emperor Huanzong was deposed by his cousin Anquan (Xiangzong).</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Toghrul</b>, sworn brother of Temüjin's father, became the most powerful khan of central Mongolia and received the title Ong Khan from the Jin dynasty (1115-1234) in 1196 after the campaign against the Tatars.</blockquote><a href="/state/keraite-khanate">Keraite Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Secret History of the Mongols, Temüjin's father Yesügei had stolen his wife Hö'elün from a Merkit, and in revenge the Merkits abducted Temüjin's bride Börte, who was rescued with help from <b>Toghrul</b> of the Keraite Khanate and Jamukha.</blockquote><a href="/state/merkit-confederation">Merkit confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1196 the Jin, with Temüjin and the Keraite ruler <b>Toghrul</b>, defeated the Tatar chief Megüjin Seultü.</blockquote><a href="/state/tatar-confederation">Tatar confederation</a></li></ul></section>
